  "explanation": "You've already chained steps, like df.groupby(...).mean().round(2). Pros write whole analyses as one readable chain. Wrap it in round brackets so each step can sit on its own line. assign() adds a new column inside a chain: .assign(with_tip=lambda d: d[\"total\"] + d[\"tip\"]). The lambda d means 'the table at this point in the chain'. Chains read top to bottom like a recipe: filter, then add, then sort, then show.",
  "example": {
    "code": "(\n    df.dropna(subset=[\"tip\"])\n    .assign(with_tip=lambda d: d[\"total\"] + d[\"tip\"])\n    .sort_values(\"with_tip\", ascending=False)\n    .head(3)\n)",
    "lines": [
